The Euclidean distances in the embedded space are computed as
\begin{equation}
 d^{\text{geo}}_{ij} = \| \mathbf{x}_i - \mathbf{x}_j \| \,.
\end{equation}
Here, $d^{\text{geo}}_{ij}$ represents the geometric distance in the embedded space, whereas $d^{\text{top}}_{ij}$ is the topological distance computed from the original network.

design.tex:
For the connected undirected analysis graph, let \(\delta_{ij}=d_G(i,j)\) denote the finite shortest-path distance between nodes \(i\) and \(j\), computed from strictly positive edge lengths \(\ell_{uv}\). We use the metric-stress objective
\begin{equation}
F_{\Delta,w}(X)
=
\sum_{i<j}w_{ij}
\left(\delta_{ij}-\|\mathbf x_i-\mathbf x_j\|\right)^2,
\qquad w_{ij}=1.
\label{eq:stress_function}
\end{equation}

Classical (Torgerson--Gower) MDS can be expressed via spectral decomposition~\cite{borg2005modern}. Let the squared dissimilarity matrix be
\begin{equation}
 \mathbf{G} = -\frac{1}{2} H \mathcal{D}^{(2)} H \,,
\end{equation}
where $H = I - \frac{1}{n}\mathbf{1}\mathbf{1}^{\mathsf T}$ is the centering matrix. Performing the eigendecomposition $\mathbf{G} = U \Lambda U^{\mathsf T}$ and retaining the top $p$ components yields
\begin{equation}
 X = U_p \Lambda_p^{1/2}.
 \label{eq:top_p_eigenvalue}
\end{equation}

protocol.tex:
When $d_{ij}(X^{(t)})=0$, we replace it with a small divergence guard $\varepsilon_{0}$ (we use $\varepsilon_{0}=10^{-5}$) to avoid numerical instabilities in the construction of $B(X^{(t)})$; this is distinct from the SMACOF convergence tolerance \texttt{eps}$=10^{-6}$ used as the stopping criterion. We then apply a SMACOF majorization step with uniform weights $w_{ij}=1$ and re-center the configuration after each iteration so that $\sum_{i=1}^n x_i^{(t)}=0$. Under this convention, the update can be written as
\begin{equation}
 X^{(t+1)}=\frac{1}{n}\,B\!\left(X^{(t)}\right)X^{(t)}.
 \label{notation: convention not}
\end{equation}
For uniform weights $w_{ij}=1$, this update coincides with the Guttman transform $X^{(t+1)}=V^{+}B(X^{(t)})X^{(t)}$, where $V^{+}=\tfrac{1}{n}H$ is the Moore--Penrose inverse of the weight Laplacian $V=nH$; since $B(X^{(t)})X^{(t)}$ is already centered, the projection $H$ acts as the identity and the update reduces to Eq.~\eqref{notation: convention not}.
The stress $F(X)$ is recomputed after each update. The SMACOF majorization ensures a non-increasing stress sequence, i.e., $F(X^{(t+1)})\le F(X^{(t)})$, until convergence, maximum of 300 iterations.

While classical MDS yields an embedding through eigendecomposition, SMACOF provides an iterative stress-reduction procedure that can converge to different local minima, depending on the initialization. This property is essential for our definition of NEI, which quantifies embedding variability across an ensemble of initializations.

Initial coordinates are drawn uniformly from the bounded support $\Omega_0=[0,1]^{N\times p}$ used by the implementation. The probability that a run terminates in $\mathcal{B}_\gamma$ is then the fraction of $\Omega_0$ that basin occupies,
\begin{equation}
 P_\gamma=\frac{\mathrm{Vol}\!\left(\mathcal{B}_\gamma\cap\Omega_0\right)}{\mathrm{Vol}(\Omega_0)},
 \qquad\sum_\gamma P_\gamma=1,
 \label{eq:basin_occupancy}
\end{equation}
with $\mathrm{Vol}(\cdot)$ the Lebesgue measure on $\mathbb{R}^{N\times p}$. The restriction to $\Omega_0$ is not a technicality: the basins are unbounded along the translation directions, so $\mathrm{Vol}(\mathcal{B}_\gamma)$ is not normalizable on $\mathbb{R}^{N\times p}$ and the occupancy $\{P_\gamma\}$ is defined only relative to a specified initialization measure. NEI is accordingly a property of the pair (topology, sampling protocol) — which is precisely the operational claim we make, and the reason all networks and null models are sampled under an identical protocol.

schoenberg.tex:
Classical (Torgerson--Gower) MDS can be expressed via spectral decomposition~\cite{borg2005modern}. Let the squared dissimilarity matrix be
\begin{equation}
 \mathbf{G} = -\frac{1}{2} H \mathcal{D}^{(2)} H \,,
\end{equation}
where $H = I - \frac{1}{n}\mathbf{1}\mathbf{1}^{\mathsf T}$ is the centering matrix. Performing the eigendecomposition $\mathbf{G} = U \Lambda U^{\mathsf T}$ and retaining the top $p$ components yields
\begin{equation}
 X = U_p \Lambda_p^{1/2}.
 \label{eq:top_p_eigenvalue}
\end{equation}
This formulation clarifies that MDS coordinates are not arbitrary visualization outputs but approximate Euclidean realizations of topology-derived dissimilarities. In this sense, the embedding is directly tied to the network’s relational structure, which makes repeated realizations of the same optimization problem physically interpretable.

