shape.tex:
In the full coordinate space, \(\nabla^2 F\succ0\) is impossible because translation and rotation generate zero modes. If a local-minimum definition is genuinely needed later, use this only after computing the projected Hessian:
\begin{equation}
\mathcal L_{G,\vartheta}
=
\left\{[X]\in\mathbb R^{N\times p}/\mathrm E(p):
X\ \text{is a strict local minimizer of }F_{G,\vartheta}\right\},
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}
\mathrm E(p)=\mathbb R^p\rtimes O(p).
\label{eq:euclidean_group}
\end{equation}

terminal.tex:
Initial coordinates are drawn uniformly from the bounded support $\Omega_0=[0,1]^{N\times p}$ used by the implementation. The probability that a run terminates in $\mathcal{B}_\gamma$ is then the fraction of $\Omega_0$ that basin occupies,
\begin{equation}
 P_\gamma=\frac{\mathrm{Vol}\!\left(\mathcal{B}_\gamma\cap\Omega_0\right)}{\mathrm{Vol}(\Omega_0)},
 \qquad\sum_\gamma P_\gamma=1,
 \label{eq:basin_occupancy}
\end{equation}
with $\mathrm{Vol}(\cdot)$ the Lebesgue measure on $\mathbb{R}^{N\times p}$. The restriction to $\Omega_0$ is not a technicality: the basins are unbounded along the translation directions, so $\mathrm{Vol}(\mathcal{B}_\gamma)$ is not normalizable on $\mathbb{R}^{N\times p}$ and the occupancy $\{P_\gamma\}$ is defined only relative to a specified initialization measure. NEI is accordingly a property of the pair (topology, sampling protocol) — which is precisely the operational claim we make, and the reason all networks and null models are sampled under an identical protocol.

If each run terminates in the basin containing its initialization, the $M$ runs are i.i.d.\ draws from the occupancy measure $\{P_\gamma\}$ of Eq.~\eqref{eq:basin_occupancy}, and by the strong law of large numbers, the empirical statistics of Eq.~\eqref{eq:pairwise_statistics} converge almost surely,
\begin{equation}
 \overline{d}_{ij}\;\xrightarrow[M\to\infty]{\mathrm{a.s.}}\;
 \mu_{ij}\equiv\sum_{\gamma}P_\gamma\,d_{ij}(X_\gamma^\star),
 \label{eq:basin_mean}
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}
 \sigma_{ij}^{2}\;\xrightarrow[M\to\infty]{\mathrm{a.s.}}\;
 s_{ij}^{2}\equiv\sum_{\gamma}P_\gamma\left[d_{ij}(X_\gamma^\star)-\mu_{ij}\right]^{2},
 \label{eq:inter_basin_variance}
\end{equation}
 At finite $M$, the estimator in Eq.~\eqref{eq:pairwise_statistics} is the biased (maximum-likelihood) variance, $\langle\sigma_{ij}^{2}\rangle=(1-1/M)\,s_{ij}^{2}$; at $M=100$ this is a uniform $1\%$ downward bias that cancels in every comparison, since all networks and null models are evaluated at the same $M$. Convergence in $M$ is verified in Fig.~\ref{fig:saturation}.

hessian.tex:
At a terminal configuration $X^{\star}$ the second variation of the stress
[Eq.~\eqref{eq:stress_function}] is block structured. Writing
$u_{ij}=(x_i-x_j)/d_{ij}$ for the unit vector along a pair and $\mathbb{1}_p$
for the $p\times p$ identity,
%
\begin{equation}
  B_{ij} \;=\; 2\,w_{ij}\Big[\,
     u_{ij}u_{ij}^{\mathsf{T}}
     \;+\;\Big(1-\frac{\delta_{ij}}{d_{ij}}\Big)
        \big(\mathbb{1}_p-u_{ij}u_{ij}^{\mathsf{T}}\big)\Big],
  \label{eq:hess_block}
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}
  H_{ii}=\sum_{j\neq i}B_{ij},
  \qquad
  H_{ij}=-B_{ij}\quad (i\neq j),
  \label{eq:hessian}
\end{equation}
%
so $H$ is a graph Laplacian whose edge weights are the $p\times p$ blocks
$B_{ij}$.

