NEI.tex:
For each embedding run \(m\in\{1,\dots,M\}\), we obtain node
coordinates \(\{\mathbf{r}^{(m)}_i\}_{i=1}^{N}\) and pairwise
Euclidean distances
\[
d^{(m)}_{ij}
=
\left\|
\mathbf{r}^{(m)}_i-\mathbf{r}^{(m)}_j
\right\|.
\]
We define the ensemble mean and variance by
\begin{equation}
\bar d_{ij}
\equiv
\frac{1}{M}\sum_{m=1}^{M}d^{(m)}_{ij},
\qquad
\sigma_{ij}^{2}
\equiv
\frac{1}{M}\sum_{m=1}^{M}
\left(d^{(m)}_{ij}-\bar d_{ij}\right)^{2}.
\label{eq:pairwise_statistics}
\end{equation}

Because the coefficient-of-variation form is undefined when
\(\bar d_{ij}=0\), we restrict the relative pairwise variability
to node pairs with a strictly positive ensemble-mean distance.
Let
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{P}_{+}
\equiv
\left\{
(i,j):1\le i<j\le N,\ \bar d_{ij}>0
\right\},
\qquad
Q\equiv |\mathcal{P}_{+}|.
\label{eq:admissible_pairs}
\end{equation}
For each \((i,j)\in\mathcal{P}_{+}\), we define
\begin{equation}
r_{ij}
\equiv
\left(
\frac{\sigma_{ij}}{\bar d_{ij}}
\right)^{2}.
\label{eq:rel_pair}
\end{equation}
The average network embedding inconsistency is then
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{I}
\equiv
\frac{1}{Q}
\sum_{(i,j)\in\mathcal{P}_{+}}r_{ij}.
\label{eq:NEI}
\end{equation}

Pairs with \(\bar d_{ij}=0\) are excluded because their relative
variability is undefined; no arbitrary regularization constant is
introduced. We report the number of excluded pairs for each network.
If \(Q=0\), \(\mathcal{I}\) is undefined. For \(Q>0\), the definition
is invariant under a global rescaling
\(d^{(m)}_{ij}\mapsto a\,d^{(m)}_{ij}\) with \(a>0\).

robust.tex:
\caption{Convergence of the average NEI with ensemble size, for the $80$ of the $84$ empirical networks whose sweep is complete under the unified protocol. (a) Each grey curve is one network, divided by its own
$\mathcal{I}(100)$; the blue curve and band are the median and interquartile range. Curves are running estimates taken from prefixes of a single ensemble of $100$ embeddings, so the endpoint is $\mathcal{I}(100)$ by construction. (b) The fraction of networks whose estimate lies within $5\%$ (vermillion squares, lower curve) and $10\%$ (orange triangles, upper curve) of that endpoint. The median converges quickly but the spread across networks does not: the median curve is within $2\%$ of its endpoint by $M=40$ and within $1\%$ by $M=70$, yet $40\%$ of networks remain outside the $5\%$ band at $M=50$ and $5\%$ at $M=90$. The networks that converge slowest are those with the smallest $\mathcal{I}$, whose $r_{ij}$ distribution is dominated by a thin tail that an ensemble of $100$ samples sparsely; for these, $\mathcal{I}$ is
quoted with its bootstrap interval.}

split.tex:
Given a grouping $\{\gamma\}$ with occupancies $P_{\gamma}$ and group means
$\mu^{\gamma}_{ij}$, the ensemble variance of a pair separates as
%
\begin{equation}
  \sigma^{2}_{ij}
  \;=\;
  \underbrace{\sum_{\gamma}P_{\gamma}\big(\mu^{\gamma}_{ij}-\bar d_{ij}\big)^{2}}
    _{\text{between}}
  \;+\;
  \underbrace{\sum_{\gamma}P_{\gamma}\,
     \mathrm{Var}_{\gamma}\!\big[d_{ij}\big]}_{\text{within}} ,
  \label{eq:var_decomp}
\end{equation}
%
the between term being the empirical counterpart of the two-configuration
expression Eq.~\eqref{eq:two_basin} without the restriction to two groups. We
write $\beta$ for the ratio of the summed between term to the summed total over
the admissible pairs $\mathcal{P}_{+}$.
